# Guidance for settings-overlay.json, beside this file. JSON has no comments, so it is here.
#
# setup.sh builds ~/.claude/settings.json from this repo's base settings with that overlay on
# top. Objects merge key by key, and any other value replaces the base's, arrays included. A
# copy of `permissions.allow` must therefore list every rule you want. Left as `{}`, the
# overlay changes nothing.
#
# To fill in: add your own keys to settings-overlay.json, as strict JSON. An example, with what
# each key does after it. Copy only the keys you want, and change the values:
#
# --- example begins
#   {
#     "model": "sonnet",
#     "theme": "dark",
#     "permissions": {
#       "allow": ["Bash(git status)", "Bash(git diff:*)", "Read(*)"],
#       "deny": ["Read(./.env)", "Read(./secrets/**)"]
#     },
#     "env": { "EXAMPLE_VARIABLE": "1" }
#   }
# --- example ends
#
#   model        the model a new session starts with
#   theme        the terminal colors Claude Code uses
#   permissions  allow: what runs without asking you; deny: what is refused even if allowed.
#                Each rule is a tool name with an optional pattern in parentheses
#   env          environment variables set in every session
#
# Keep anything secret out of this file, since it lives in a repo: no tokens or keys in "env".
#
# To check: run setup.sh, then read ~/.claude/settings.json. If you changed that file directly,
# setup.sh shows the difference and stops. Move the change to the overlay and run it again, or
# pass --force-settings to discard it.
